#lang saffron
;; saffron-lang-plan D6: a refinement in a Saffron file becomes a RUNTIME
;; CONTRACT, checked -- not an error, and not a silent drop.
;;
;; `#refine{x : int | (> x 0)}` needs a static base type and an SMT discharge,
;; and an `any` has neither.  The language already ships the dynamic answer
;; (stdlib/contract.tur), so the predicate stays in the source where a reader
;; can see it and is checked at run time.  The plan is explicit that the one
;; thing which must NOT happen is a refinement being accepted and IGNORED --
;; that would make a Saffron file look like it carries a guarantee it does not.
;;
;; The INTERPRETER already did this correctly.  The compiled path emitted
;; uncompilable C:
;;
;;   error: incompatible type for argument 1 of 'tur_contract_check'
;;          ... tur_tagged_t
;;
;; because a predicate over an `any` is a dyn-op returning `any` while
;; `tur-contract-check` takes a `bool`.  D4's truthiness is the same answer
;; `if` already uses for the same question, so the two now share one helper
;; (`elab_saffron_truthy`) rather than the contract path growing its own.
;;
;; This is the PASSING half; tests/fixtures/saffron-refinement-violated is the
;; other one.  A fixture that only checked the passing case would also pass if
;; the predicate were dropped entirely, which is exactly the failure D6 warns
;; about -- so the pair is the assertion, not either half alone.

(defn pos [x : #refine{v : any | (> v 0)}] x)

(defn main []
  (println (cast (pos (:: 5 any)) int))
  (println (cast (pos (:: 1 any)) int))
  0)
